## Planning this task

You decide how this task is split, and you write no product code.

Read the task, work out which parts of this repo it touches, and write your plan
to `{{.Role.PlanPath}}`, naming one strand per part. Then stop and say what you
concluded. Landing this strand is what creates them, so nothing happens until a
person has read your plan.

That path is outside the repository on purpose, and nothing about this plan
belongs inside it: a file written in the worktree ends up linted, formatted or
committed by tooling that has never heard of norn. Commit nothing here.

```yaml
summary: one line on how you split it and why
strands:
  - role: <short-name>        # becomes a branch segment and the name on the rail
    agent: claude             # optional; the repo's default agent otherwise
    model: opus               # optional
    after: <other-role>       # optional; starts only once that strand has landed
    expect: red               # optional; red or green, checked when it lands
    brief: |
      What this strand owns, in your words: which files, which service, which
      part of the task. Name what it must not touch, since the others are
      working at the same time.
```

Rules that make a plan work:

- One strand per part that can be worked on independently. Two strands editing
  the same files is a merge conflict you chose in advance.
- Every strand's brief names its files or its service. A strand that only knows
  its own name will guess, and it will guess wide.
- Use `after:` when the order is real (a test that must exist before the code it
  describes), not to be tidy: strands run in parallel, and that is the point.
- Fewer strands than parts is fine. More strands than there is work for is not.
